WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:04.830
Horror movies are all about pushing boundaries,
taking audiences and ratings boards to the

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.500
very limit of what they can stomach.

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:11.420
Some films have been so hardcore that entire
countries have taken steps to ban them from

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:15.800
their theaters, in some cases making screening
or possessing them a criminal act.

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:19.880
Sometimes you can see the rationale behind
a movie being banned, but not always.

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.490
For one reason or another, these horror films
were all battlegrounds for censorship, deemed

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:28.420
too disturbing for viewers… but they still
managed to find their audiences anyway.

00:00:28.940 --> 00:00:29.840
A Serbian Film

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:34.910
A Serbian Film has a reputation as one of
the most extreme horror movies ever made,

00:00:34.910 --> 00:00:36.540
and it's a reputation that's well-earned.

00:00:36.820 --> 00:00:41.960
Centering on an adult film actor who's been
hired to participate in an increasingly sadistic

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:46.320
production, the story is meant to be a metaphor
for life in Serbia, conveying how the country

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.080
screws its people over from birth.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.960
That metaphor gets very literal, being represented
on screen by torture, murder, necrophilia,

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:55.620
and other, even worse things.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.840
With that kind of taboo content, it's far
from surprising that a large number of countries

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:01.540
wanted nothing to do with the movie.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:06.780
At one point or another, A Serbian Film was
banned in Germany, Norway, Brazil, Australia,

00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.430
New Zealand, Malaysia, Spain, and Singapore,
and the countries that eventually allowed

00:01:11.430 --> 00:01:13.380
the movie required significant edits.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:17.400
The movie is so extreme that it's almost gotten
people thrown in real-life prison.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.780
Following A Serbian Film's ban in Spain, the
director of a film festival faced criminal

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.370
charges for daring to show the movie anyway,
a move which drew condemnation from the film's

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:26.900
director.

00:01:27.220 --> 00:01:32.240
Despite how violent the film is, it uses its
extreme violence to make a point, and of course,

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.740
no children were actually harmed during the
making of it.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:39.940
A Serbian Film may be one of the most boundary-pushing
movies ever made, but it's still just a movie,

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:42.600
and as a horror movie, it's pretty genuinely
effective.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.880
Saw VI &amp; Saw 3D

00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:49.930
The Saw series of horror movies was sadistic
from the start, but weirdly, it wasn't until

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.950
the sixth entry that any country made moves
to ban them.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:57.970
For some reason, Saw VI was initially banned
from Thailand and Spain, with Spanish regulators

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.580
giving the film a rating usually reserved
for pornography.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:04.980
It's an odd move, considering the sixth movie
doesn't do anything the previous five didn't

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:06.020
already get away with.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:11.740
The follow-up picture, alternatively called
Saw 3D, Saw 7, or Saw: The Final Chapter,

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.880
was also banned from public exhibition in
Germany for its violence.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.500
But what makes that movie more violent than
the installments that preceded it is a mystery.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.610
The seventh Saw movie was kind of a cornball
gimmicky sequel lousy enough to put the franchise

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.160
into hibernation for years.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.400
Hostel &amp; Hostel: Part II

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.660
Lots of European commentators had an issue
with the first Hostel for making the continent

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:33.060
seem like a lethal tourist trap.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:37.020
But it was only in Ukraine that the movie
pushed enough buttons to get itself banned.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.700
Both Hostel and its sequel were outlawed in
the country for excessive cruelty, as well

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:45.300
as for portraying the neighborhood as a place
where tourists get tortured for money.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:49.300
Despite the ban, the movie is still legally
available for private viewing, just don't

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.140
let them catch you screening it in the front
yard.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:56.100
Beyond Ukraine, the uncut version of Hostel:
Part II is banned in Germany and New Zealand,

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:00.270
and the film was only released in Malaysia
and Singapore after undergoing cuts to its

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:01.460
more extreme scenes.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:06.260
Some politicians in the U.K. have also argued
that the film should be illegal, a crusade

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:10.720
made extra absurd by the fact that at least
one politician who spoke out against the movie

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:13.100
eventually admitted that he'd never even watched
it.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.580
It's a fact that speaks to how arbitrary these
censorship measures often are, one viewer's

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:20.220
trash is another audience's trashy good time.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:21.560
Possession

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:25.820
Possession is one of the best and most bonkers
horror movies ever made, but that doesn't

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:29.820
mean everyone was comfortable with its content
when it was released in 1981.

00:03:29.940 --> 00:03:34.680
Following a limited theatrical run in the
U.K., Possession was labeled as a distasteful

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:39.099
"video nasty" and effectively banned for a
decade, not getting released on home video

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:41.580
in uncut form until 1999.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:46.000
The movie also had a difficult time getting
an uncut release in the United States.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.860
While it was never formally banned, the original
U.S. release was heavily edited, resulting

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.440
in a number of different versions being circulated
with dramatic variations.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.660
One version notoriously cut out an astonishing
40 minutes, rearranging scenes and adding

00:03:59.660 --> 00:04:04.480
optical effects that turned an already challenging
movie into something all-but-impossible to

00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:05.000
understand.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:10.080
It wasn't until 2000 that the original version
became widely available in the U.S., which

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.900
is shocking, considering the movie's relatively
tame nature.

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:17.459
While its plot may feel like a fever dream,
it's no more violent or graphic than other

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:18.620
horror movies of the era.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:20.260
It's just a lot weirder.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:21.460
Land of the Dead

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.640
Banning a movie instantly makes it more notorious.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:29.340
Most authorities tend to do it only in extreme
circumstances, with movies so extreme that

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.780
they'd test the mettle of even the most jaded
viewer.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:36.550
So it's odd that a relatively tame studio
effort like George Romero's Land of the Dead

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:40.629
ended up being banned in Ukraine, especially
when more typically-restrictive places like

00:04:40.629 --> 00:04:43.300
the U.K. classified the movie as being suitable
for teenagers.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.800
The movie's ban in Ukraine comes down to unique
context; specifically, it was banned for its

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:50.200
portrayal of cannibalism.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:55.140
Not because its scenes of humans getting devoured
were especially gory, but rather due to concerns

00:04:55.159 --> 00:04:58.969
that such content would evoke memories of
the Holodomor, a man-made famine that killed

00:04:58.969 --> 00:05:03.650
millions when it was inflicted on the nation
by Joseph Stalin's government between 1932

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:04.340
and '33.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:07.520
The reason behind the ban raises more questions
than it answers.

00:05:07.990 --> 00:05:12.680
Land of the Dead was released in 2005, some
72 years after the events of the genocide,

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:16.040
and doesn't have anything to do with famine
or Soviet history.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:20.930
You can almost see the rationale, but is a
zombie movie that takes place in post-apocalyptic

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:24.260
Pennsylvania really going to be the thing
that opens those old wounds?

00:05:24.820 --> 00:05:28.440
It's one of George Romero's worst movies,
sure, but it's not that bad.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:29.840
Grotesque

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:35.639
2009's Grotesque is a 73-minute exploitation
horror movie from Japan that focuses chiefly

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:38.440
on the kidnapping and sadistic murder of a
young couple.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:39.040
That's it.

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:39.960
Nothing else happens.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:43.000
Grotesque is the dictionary definition of
torture porn.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:48.159
The U.K.'s film classification board refused
to put any stamp of approval on the film,

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.960
stating that the movie actually posed a risk
to society.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:56.300
Running down the movie's main offenses, the
censors cited acts of "amputation, eye gouging,

00:05:56.309 --> 00:06:00.610
castration, and evisceration resulting in
a gory and violent death", in other words,

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:03.440
a typical horror movie fan's definition of
a fun Friday night.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:07.840
The lack of a traditional narrative only bolstered
the board's decision to reject the movie,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:11.339
with their assessment being that the film
could not be cut to satisfy their standards

00:06:11.340 --> 00:06:13.580
when its very concept was so objectionable.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:18.160
Grotesque's home country had no such concerns,
although the British ban did spark a debate

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:20.180
over the artistic merits of the splatter film.

00:06:20.500 --> 00:06:24.520
The controversy brought a wave of new publicity
to the movie, suggesting once again that there's

00:06:24.520 --> 00:06:28.770
nothing so valuable for a movie's long-term
reputation as the allure it gains from being

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:29.260
banned.

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:30.600
Father's Day

00:06:30.940 --> 00:06:35.639
Described as "pure grindhouse madness," Father's
Day is a Canadian horror-comedy about a man

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:38.560
getting revenge on the killer who molested
and murdered his father.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:43.240
The outlandish concept is clearly tongue-in-cheek
in execution, not that that mattered to the

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:44.380
Australian government.

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:49.120
Although Father's Day was allowed to screen
in Sydney for its 2012 theatrical premiere,

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.720
classification was refused for its home video
release, resulting in an effective ban on

00:06:53.720 --> 00:06:57.780
the movie in the country, with future screenings
of it at film festivals being canceled.

00:06:58.210 --> 00:07:02.080
With the film banned from a video release,
a distributor for Father's Day took Australian

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:05.200
censors to task for taking the movie's content
out of context.

00:07:05.420 --> 00:07:10.060
After much back-and-forth in the press, the
movie was eventually released in 2013, leaving

00:07:10.060 --> 00:07:11.940
a censored version that toned down the gore.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:13.500
Slender Man

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:17.990
Sony Pictures' Slender Man is the first studio
movie to be inspired by the online horror

00:07:17.990 --> 00:07:21.830
phenomenon, telling the story of a group of
teenage girls who seek out the mysterious

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:22.320
monster.

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:23.940
At least, that was the plan.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:28.710
Prior to its release, Slender Man sparked
controversy due to its relationship to a real-life

00:07:28.710 --> 00:07:33.530
attempted murder that took place near Waukesha,
Wisconsin in 2014, when two middle school

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:37.580
students violently attacked their friend in
an effort to appease the fictional creature.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:42.020
In the lead-up to the movie's theatrical rollout,
the father of one of the attackers condemned

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:46.039
the movie, accusing its producers of capitalizing
on a tragedy, saying,

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:48.520
"In my opinion it's extremely distasteful.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.980
All we're doing is extending the pain all
three of these families have gone through."

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:56.499
Reportedly, the negative buzz spooked Sony
into dramatically chopping down the movie,

00:07:56.500 --> 00:08:00.500
removing much of the film's violence, as well
as plot arcs and entire characters.

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.700
But for some exhibitors, that self-censorship
wasn't enough, with theaters near the site

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.860
of the attack refusing to screen the movie
at all.

00:08:07.069 --> 00:08:10.800
The result of all the drama was an incoherent
mess of a movie that got little in the way

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:14.300
of a marketing push, getting poorly-received
by just about everyone.

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:18.279
The most puzzling thing is that anyone could
have seen the controversy coming from a mile

00:08:18.280 --> 00:08:21.720
away, which makes one wonder why producers
even tried to make the movie in the first

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:22.220
place.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:23.800
The Bunny Game

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:29.800
An independent 76-minute movie produced for
$13,000, The Bunny Game easily ran afoul of

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:32.480
British censors upon its release in 2010.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:37.099
Short, slight, and brutal, the movie centers
on the kidnapping and torture of a prostitute

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:40.640
hitchhiker by a sadistic truck driver, nothing
more, nothing less.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:45.740
The BBFC refused classification for the movie
on the basis of its graphic depictions of

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:48.580
sexual abuse, calling the movie "unacceptable
to the public."

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:52.880
The board's decision didn't come down to any
one objectionable scene, but rather the movie's

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:53.720
very concept.

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:55.800
According to a statement made by the board,

00:08:56.140 --> 00:09:00.950
"The principal focus of the work is the unremitting
sexual and physical abuse of a helpless woman,

00:09:00.950 --> 00:09:04.640
as well as the sadistic and sexual pleasure
the man derive[s] from this."

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.900
In short, there may be no version of The Bunny
Game that British censors would find acceptable.

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:12.820
On principle alone, that's a pretty big bummer
for British fans of the horror genre.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.200
The Human Centipede 2 (Full Sequence)

00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:19.820
Despite its notoriously disgusting concept,
the first Human Centipede movie has a fairly

00:09:19.820 --> 00:09:20.960
traditional feel to it.

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:25.360
Its central idea of kidnapped people being
stitched together end-to-end is body horror

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:30.390
more nauseating in concept than in execution,
with the movie itself being fairly restrained

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:31.760
when it comes to graphic detail.

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:35.560
That restraint went out the window for the
movie's sequel, which went to extreme lengths

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:39.660
to shock and horrify, getting itself banned
from several nations upon its release.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:44.200
Stark and confrontational, The Human Centipede
2 (Full Sequence) hit the throttle on its

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:46.400
use of sadistic, nihilistic violence.

00:09:46.620 --> 00:09:50.300
While the first film's villain was a demented
surgeon who at least sort of knew what he

00:09:50.310 --> 00:09:54.620
was doing, the second movie's villain is just
demented, creating his centipede with brute

00:09:54.620 --> 00:09:55.900
force and barbed wire.

00:09:56.090 --> 00:10:00.360
The film is so bleak and brutal that the British
Board of Film Classification outright refused

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:03.930
to give it a rating, saying "no amount of
cuts" would make the movie acceptable enough

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.300
to be exhibited or sold.

00:10:05.530 --> 00:10:09.860
The movie was eventually approved for release
in the United Kingdom, but only after having

00:10:09.860 --> 00:10:11.640
two and a half minutes of footage cut out.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:15.880
Australia required 30 seconds' worth of edits,
while New Zealand never let the movie screen

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:16.660
at all.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:20.580
If you missed out on the sequel, you didn't
miss much, but it's still so much better than

00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:24.180
the third movie in the series, which censors
around the world didn't even bother with.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:26.040
That one's not terribly offensive.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:27.980
It's more just… terrible.

